<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxias I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 160 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Artashes I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxiad-dynasty">Artaxiad dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontid-dynasty">Orontid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artaxias I was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxiad-dynasty">Artaxiad dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. A general serving the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, he declared himself king after the Seleucid defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE and united the Armenian-speaking lands, setting boundary stones that still survive. He founded the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> and reigned until about 160 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxias-i.jpg" alt="Artaxias I" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leu Numismatik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Artaxias_I.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> had been ruled since the 5th century BCE by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontid-dynasty">Orontid dynasty</a>, probably of Iranian origin. At the end of the 3rd century BCE the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a> appointed Artaxias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zariadres">Zariadres</a> as military governors of Greater Armenia and Sophene. Strabo calls them Antiochus's generals, but boundary stones with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscriptions show that Artaxias called himself an Orontid king and the son of Zareh, so that he and Zariadres were members of local branches of the dynasty and not outsiders. The later historian Movses Khorenatsi says Artaxias defeated and killed the preceding king Eruand, that is Orontes, in a war for the throne, though in one scholar's view his account adapts the battle of Alexander and Darius in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance">Alexander Romance</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the Seleucid defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a>, Artaxias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zariadres">Zariadres</a> revolted and declared themselves independent kings in Greater Armenia and Sophene, and in 188 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> recognised them. They allied to expand their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>, and the Seleucid king Seleucus IV did not fight them. Artaxias's kingdom, first centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> valley, grew into Iberian land and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-atropatene">Media Atropatene</a>, and the Kura River became its northern and north-eastern border. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, the union of these lands led their people to speak the same language, Armenian, though imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> remained the language of court and government. Khorenatsi says he ordered the delimiting of villages and farmland, and about twenty boundary stones with Aramaic inscriptions found in modern Armenia confirm it.</p><p>He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>, which served as capital until the 2nd century CE and became a major administrative and commercial centre. By 179 BCE he was strong enough to mediate in a war among the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, but his plan to annex Sophene failed. In 165 or 164 BCE the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a> defeated and briefly captured him, apparently releasing him once he recognised Antiochus's authority, though his control of Greater Armenia seems not to have suffered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Artaxias died around 160 BCE and was succeeded by his son Artavasdes I. His other known son was Tigranes I. He is regarded by modern scholars as the founder of independent Armenian statehood, and the historian Hakob Manandian called him the first true organiser of Greater Armenia. He features in the Armenian folk epic Vipasank', which Khorenatsi drew on; it places in his time an invasion by the Alans that actually belongs to the 1st century CE. In 2001 a statue of him was erected in the central square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artashat</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Artaxias I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Strabo and Plutarch say Hannibal was received at the court of <b>Artaxias I</b> and, in a story they also tell, planned and supervised the building of Artaxata, though the tale is reckoned apocryphal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 200 BCE the Seleucid king Antiochus III defeated Orontes IV and divided the land between his generals <b>Artaxias</b> and Zariadres.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-orontid">Kingdom of Armenia (Orontid)</a></li></ul></section>
